---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Acquired"
  description="A personal museum for accessioning meaningful objects with photographs, provenance, catalog records, and the story of why each thing matters."
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="kh-case section kh-case-acquired" data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="kh-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="kh-case-kicker">06 / PRODUCT / ARCHIVE / COLLECTION SYSTEMS</p>
        <h1 class="kh-case-title kh-acquired-case-title">ACQUIRED</h1>
        <p class="kh-case-deck">A personal museum for the things you keep — and why you kept them.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <dl class="kh-case-meta">
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Concept / Product Design / Development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Built</dt>
            <dd>2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Core idea</dt>
            <dd>Give meaningful objects a durable record for provenance and personal significance.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <div class="kh-case-actions">
          <a
            class="kh-pill"
            href="https://iphiginea.github.io/acquired/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Open app ↗</a
          >
          <a
            class="kh-text-link"
            href="https://github.com/iphiginea/acquired"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="kh-case-body">
      <p class="kh-case-section-label">THE PROJECT</p>
      <div class="kh-case-prose">
        <h2>Why I made it</h2>
        <p>
          We save photographs by the thousands, but the context around a physical object is much
          easier to lose: where it came from, who owned it, how it was acquired, and why anyone cared
          enough to keep it. Acquired is where that context gets to survive.
        </p>

        <h2>What I built</h2>
        <p>
          Acquired treats everyday objects like museum holdings. Each object receives a permanent
          accession number and a structured record, like title, date, maker, classification,
          materials, place, acquisition history, and provenance, alongside its personal significance.
          Photographs document each piece, and every record can stay private or be made public
          individually.
        </p>

        <h2>The product logic</h2>
        <p>
          The system starts with accessioning rather than organizing. A collection code, year, and
          sequence number give every object a stable identity; the collection view makes those
          records searchable; and the individual object page keeps the full history behind the
          thumbnail. The structure is meant to remain useful as a collection grows instead of
          depending on folders, filenames, or memory.
        </p>

        <h2>Design direction</h2>
        <p>
          The product itself borrows from museum collection catalogs rather than social feeds:
          accession numbers, structured records, restrained typography, and object-focused layouts.
          The portfolio page stays within the same visual system as the rest of my work so the project
          feels like part of one body of experiments rather than a separate microsite.
        </p>

        <h2>Built to keep growing</h2>
        <p>
          Supabase handles authentication, structured object records, and image storage. The app also
          supports image cropping and browser-based background removal, so object photography can stay
          visually consistent without changing the underlying record. This isn't about owning more.
          It's about remembering why any of it mattered.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="kh-case-next">
      <a href={withBase('/works/first-light/')}>
        <span class="kh-case-section-label">PREVIOUS PROJECT</span>
        <strong>← First Light</strong>
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  @media (min-width: 981px) {
    .kh-case-acquired .kh-case-head {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(22rem, 0.7fr);
      gap: clamp(3rem, 5vw, 6rem);
    }

    .kh-acquired-case-title {
      max-width: 100%;
      font-size: clamp(5rem, 9.2vw, 10rem);
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }
</style>
